text:
The change breaks explicit-tag binary integer resolution in the Core schema. `!!int "0b101"` used to match `[-+]?0b[0-1]+`; the new `[^-+]?0b[0-1]+` alternative no longer matches (the first `0` is consumed by `[^-+]?`, leaving `b101`, which can't match `0b...`). So an explicit `!!int` on a binary literal now throws, while the JSON-schema variant still accepts it.

```json
{"defect": true, "testFile": {"path": "test/core/proof.test.mjs", "content": "import { describe, it } from 'node:test';
import assert from 'node:assert';
import { load, CORE_SCHEMA, JSON_SCHEMA } from 'js-yaml';

describe('proof', () => {
  it('fails on the patched build', () => {
    // Explicit !!int tag on a binary literal must still resolve (core schema)
    assert.strictEqual(load('!!int 0b101', { schema: CORE_SCHEMA }), 5);
    // The JSON schema still resolves the same form, showing the core regression
    assert.strictEqual(load('!!int 0b101', { schema: JSON_SCHEMA }), 5);
  });
});
"}, "note": "The altered explicit integer pattern `[^-+]?0b[0-1]+` no longer matches plain binary literals like `0b101`, so `load('!!int 0b101')` with CORE_SCHEMA now throws 'cannot resolve a node' where it previously returned 5."}
```

